# Git line-ending pins that are part of a byte contract.
#
# DR-75: the versioned `pre-push` gate and the shell scripts around it are shell
# programs, so their line endings are part of their correctness.  This machine's
# system gitconfig (C:/Program Files/Git/etc/gitconfig) sets core.autocrlf=true,
# which would rewrite them to CRLF on checkout for anyone with that setting; a
# carriage return can break the shebang and corrupt the last token of every line
# on a shell that does not strip it.
.githooks/** -text
scripts/*.sh -text

# BATCH-B2: `.spec/bevy/PRD.md` carries a **sealed prefix** (`src/adapter/bevy/prd.rs`,
# `SEALED_PREFIX_SHA256`) whose whole point is that its bytes are frozen.  This
# machine's system gitconfig sets `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrote the file to
# CRLF on a fresh checkout (5217 B / 81 CR) while the frozen prefix is the pure-LF
# blob (5136 B), so the seal failed on a clean checkout and passed in a working
# tree that had been normalised.  Pinned to no conversion: a documented byte
# contract must survive checkout.
.spec/bevy/PRD.md -text
